  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "If $‖-\\frac{2}{3}x‖=5$, find $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}‖x‖}{15}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n‖kx‖=|k|\\cdot ‖x‖\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we can solve for the norm $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-\\frac{2}{3}x‖ & =5 \\\\ |-\\frac{2}{3}|‖x‖ & =5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}‖x‖ & =5 \\\\ ‖x‖ & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}‖x‖}{15}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}‖x‖}{15} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{15}\\cdot \\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
